## 2. Tile artwork — CC BY-SA 4.0

The 27 suit tiles in `packages/client/public/tiles/` — `man-1`…`man-9`,
`pin-1`…`pin-9`, `sou-1`…`sou-9` — come from the Wikimedia Commons category
[SVG Planar illustrations of Mahjong tiles][commons].

- **Author:** Cangjie6 (primary); Jerry Crimson Mann (original);
  User:Dewclouds (vectorisation)
- **Licence:** [CC BY-SA 4.0](https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/)
- **Changes:** the files are renamed (`MJw1-.svg` → `man-1.svg`, and so on) and
  are otherwise used as published. Per-file attribution, including each file's
  upstream name and Commons URL, is in
  [`packages/client/public/tiles/credits.json`](packages/client/public/tiles/credits.json).

`back.svg` is an original work for this project, drawn to match the dimensions
and border of the Cangjie6 set. It is MIT, like the code.

[commons]: https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Category:SVG_Planar_illustrations_of_Mahjong_tiles

---

## 3. The release binary is a combined work, and ships both licences

The standalone binaries built by `scripts/release/compile.ts` base64-embed the
whole built client — the 27 CC BY-SA tiles included — into the executable. That
is deliberate: a single self-contained file is the point of that build, and
splitting the art back out would mean shipping a folder beside every binary.

So a release binary is a combined work. **The MIT terms in section 1 cover its
code; the CC BY-SA 4.0 terms in section 2 cover the tile artwork inside it, and
travel with it.** Anyone redistributing a binary is redistributing the artwork
and takes on the section 2 attribution obligation. The binary carries the
attribution needed to satisfy it in three places, so it is never separated from
the art:

- `sichuan-mahjong --credits` prints it to the terminal,
- the in-app About screen states it in all three UI languages, and
- `/tiles/credits.json` is served with the per-file detail.

Nothing here restricts the code: the engine, server and client sources remain
MIT, and a build that omits `tiles/` carries no CC BY-SA obligation at all. The
npm package and the from-source path serve the tiles from disk as ordinary
files and are unaffected either way.
